Gateway summary and complete inventory reads
===========================================

This correction covers the OPNsense device-summary aggregate, HAProxy read
inventories, VPN status and the dedicated IPsec SAD/SPD read. It introduces no
device write or restore operation. Other vendors keep their existing contracts,
including the separate OpenWrt device-summary envelope.

Observed summary values
-----------------------
The summary reads diagnostics/system/system_information and system_time,
core/firmware/status, interfaces/overview/export, routes/gateway/status and
routing/settings/search_gateway. The firmware version comes from the nested
product record. Notification metadata from core/system/status is not system
identity or uptime.

Interface counts include unknown states separately from up and down. An
unassigned interface can have an empty configured identifier; duplicate nonempty
identifiers are inconsistent. Unknown interface states are not assumed down.

The primary WAN is the explicitly active, enabled IPv4 default gateway, or the
IPv6 default when no IPv4 default exists. No configured default or multiple
active defaults leaves selection unknown; the first returned row is not a
substitute. The firewall WAN address comes from that gateway's interface, while
the upstream router address is reported separately. Required inventory
inconsistency makes the summary unavailable.

Unmonitored gateway state is not an observed online state. Missing latency and
packet-loss samples remain absent, while a measured zero is retained. The UI
labels absent samples Unknown, without adding numeric units. It shows combined
service/VPN counters only when both values are known. This summary does not
collect CPU, storage, service inventory, tunnel health, IDS alerts or rule
counts and does not manufacture defaults for them.

Firmware update state is known only when the vendor reports an update/upgrade,
or reports no update with check metadata. An unchecked or failed check remains
unknown. These values reflect the vendor's response, not an independently
verified firmware freshness or vulnerability assessment.

Complete inventories and safe failures
-------------------------------------
HAProxy searches request all rows. The five inventories require consistent
pagination metadata, complete rows and unique nonempty identities. Selected
public fields have explicit string/boolean types. An unavailable or partial
component does not become a successful empty HAProxy overview.

WireGuard peer/server and OpenVPN instance inventories must be complete. Their
existing envelopes are retained, including nested settings. VPN secret values
are redacted at the adapter boundary and again where applicable at the API/UI.
IPsec reads use ipsec/sad/search and ipsec/spd/search. Provider errors and
malformed required payloads yield generic unavailable responses. Cancellation
propagates. Read requests and these regression fixtures do not modify gateways.

Acceptance limits
-----------------
Upstream contracts are pinned in the adjacent JSON fixture and local audit
receipts. Synthetic HTTP transport, isolated PostgreSQL/ASGI checks, page tests
and Chromium exercise the implementation, not a physical device or normal
production authentication. Inventories exceeding a vendor's returned page are
refused rather than silently described as complete; firmware-specific scale
limits and large deployed configurations still need acceptance.

The separate firmware-information, system-information and gateway-health
adapter methods are not certified by this summary correction and remain audit
work. These read results do not qualify gateway writes, backups, retention,
restore, high availability, production RTO/RPO or an enterprise SLA. Follow the
candidate support boundary and test the exact release images against the
intended device models, firmware versions and operations before deployment.
